    detailedAnswer: 'localStorage is accessible to any JavaScript on the page, so a single XSS exfiltrates the token. An httpOnly cookie is invisible to JS, which removes that path — but cookies are attached automatically, so you reintroduce CSRF and need SameSite=Lax/Strict or an anti-CSRF token. The standard shape is a short-lived access token (~15 minutes) held in memory and a long-lived refresh token in an httpOnly cookie, rotated on every use so a stolen token is detectable on reuse. Nothing secret ever goes in the payload: a JWT is signed, not encrypted, and anyone can base64-decode it.',
