    detailedAnswer: 'Merge records history as it happened, including the branch topology, at the cost of a noisier graph. Rebase replays commits onto a new base, producing a linear history that is far easier to read and bisect — but the replayed commits are new objects with new hashes. If someone else has already pulled those commits, their history and yours diverge and reconciling it is painful. Hence the rule: rebase before you share, merge after. To undo a commit that is already public, use git revert, which adds a new commit rather than rewriting the old one.',
