    slug: 'mongo-embed-vs-reference',
    category: 'MongoDB',
    question: 'When do you embed data and when do you reference it?',
    shortAnswer: 'Embed what you always read together and that stays bounded. Reference what is shared, unbounded, or changes independently.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'Jo hamesha saath padha jata hai aur bounded rehta hai use embed karo. Jo shared hai, bina limit badhta hai, ya alag se badalta hai use reference karo.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The decision follows the access pattern, not the relationship. Embed when the child has no life without the parent and the array is bounded — order items are the classic case, and embedding makes the whole order one atomic write. Reference when many parents share the data (a product in 10,000 orders), when it grows without limit (an activity log), or when the document would approach the 16 MB cap. That cap is the detail that matters most: an unbounded embedded array works fine in development and fails in production. Duplication is often correct here — storing the price inside an order item is a historical record of what the customer actually paid, not a normalisation bug.',
    detailedAnswerHi: 'Faisla access pattern se hota hai, relation se nahi. Embed tab jab child parent ke bina exist na kare aur array bounded ho — order items classic case hai, aur embed karne se poora order ek atomic write ban jata hai. Reference tab jab kai parents wahi data share karein (ek product 10,000 orders mein), jab wo bina limit badhe (activity log), ya jab document 16 MB cap ke paas pahunche. Wahi cap sabse important detail hai: bina limit ka embedded array development mein theek chalta hai aur production mein fail hota hai. Yahan duplication aksar sahi hai — order item mein price rakhna historical record hai ki customer ne kya diya, normalisation ka bug nahi.',
    codeExample: `// embed: bounded + read together + price is historical
{ _id, userId: ObjectId("..."), items: [{ productId, name, price, qty }], total }

// ❌ unbounded — will hit 16 MB
{ _id: userId, activityLog: [ /* forever */ ] }`,
