    slug: 'mongo-aggregation-match-first',
    category: 'MongoDB',
    question: 'What is the aggregation pipeline, and why should $match come first?',
    shortAnswer: 'A sequence of stages, each transforming documents and passing them on. $match first because only a leading $match can use an index, and every later stage then handles fewer documents.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'Stages ka ek sequence, har stage documents ko badal kar aage bhejta hai. $match pehle isliye kyunki index sirf shuru wale $match mein chalta hai, aur aage ke har stage ko phir kam documents milte hain.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Stages run in order: $match filters, $group aggregates, $sort orders, $project shapes, $lookup joins, $unwind expands an array into one document per element. Because they are sequential, filtering early is both an index win and a volume win — a $match after $group is Mongo\'s HAVING and is genuinely more expensive. $unwind deserves care: it multiplies your document count, so filter before it, never after. And $lookup is not a real join — without an index on the foreign field it becomes a query per input document, which is usually a sign the data should have been embedded.',
    detailedAnswerHi: 'Stages order mein chalte hain: $match filter, $group aggregate, $sort order, $project shape, $lookup join, $unwind array ko har element ke alag document mein todta hai. Sequential hone ki wajah se jaldi filter karna index ka bhi fayda hai aur volume ka bhi — $group ke baad ka $match Mongo ka HAVING hai aur sach mein mehenga hai. $unwind par dhyan chahiye: ye document count badha deta hai, isliye usse pehle filter karo, baad mein nahi. Aur $lookup asli join nahi hai — foreign field par index na ho to har input document ke liye ek query ban jata hai, jo aksar signal hai ki data embed karna chahiye tha.',
    followUps: ['What does $unwind do?', 'Is $lookup the same as JOIN?', 'How do you do HAVING?'],
