    question: 'Does MongoDB support ACID transactions?',
    shortAnswer: 'Yes. Single-document writes are always atomic, and multi-document ACID transactions exist since 4.0 — but they need a replica set and cost performance.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'Haan. Single-document writes hamesha atomic hain, aur 4.0 se multi-document ACID transactions bhi hain — par unke liye replica set chahiye aur performance ki keemat lagti hai.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The important half is that a single document update is atomic no matter how deeply nested it is. That is a design lever: model whatever must change together as one document and you never need a transaction at all. Multi-document transactions do exist, but they require a replica set (a standalone mongod cannot run them, though Atlas always can), they are measurably slower, and they have a 60-second default limit. Reaching for one is often a hint the schema should have embedded instead. Related: write concern w:"majority" is what makes an acknowledged write survive a failover — w:1 can be lost when the primary dies.',
    detailedAnswerHi: 'Zyada important baat ye hai ki ek document ka update kitna bhi nested ho, atomic hi hota hai. Ye ek design lever hai: jo saath badalna chahiye use ek document mein model karo, transaction ki zarurat hi nahi padegi. Multi-document transactions hain, par unke liye replica set chahiye (akela mongod nahi chala sakta, Atlas hamesha chala sakta hai), wo naapne layak dheeme hain, aur 60-second ki default limit hai. Inki zarurat padna aksar ishara hai ki schema mein embed karna chahiye tha. Isse juda: w:"majority" write concern hi acknowledged write ko failover mein bachata hai — w:1 primary marne par kho sakta hai.',
