    detailedAnswer: 'Postgres enforces correctness in the database — foreign keys, constraints, multi-row transactions — which you cannot reliably reproduce in application code. That makes it the right default for orders, payments and inventory. Mongo is strong when you know the access pattern up front, documents are read and written whole, and records genuinely differ in shape; sharding is also built in rather than a project. Two things make the answer less binary than people expect: Postgres has JSONB, so "I need flexible fields" alone is rarely a reason to leave SQL, and Mongo has had ACID transactions since 4.0. Plenty of real systems run both — Postgres for money, Mongo for the catalog.',
