    shortAnswer: 'The default list is created once at definition time and shared by every call, so results accumulate across calls.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'Wo default list sirf ek baar, definition ke waqt banti hai aur har call use share karti hai, isliye results ek call se doosri mein jud jaate hain.',
    detailedAnswer: 'Python evaluates default arguments when the def statement executes, not on each call, so a mutable default becomes shared state on the function object. Two independent calls that omit the argument will see each other\'s data. The fix is acc=None with a None check inside. The same trap applies to {} and set(), and to datetime.now() as a default, which freezes to import time. You can see the bound default on f.__defaults__.',
