    slug: 'python-gil-q',
    category: 'Python',
    question: 'What is the GIL and when does it matter?',
    shortAnswer: 'Only one thread executes Python bytecode at a time, so threads help I/O-bound work but not CPU-bound work — use multiprocessing for that.',
    shortAnswerHi: 'Ek waqt par sirf ek thread Python bytecode chalata hai, isliye threads I/O-bound kaam mein madad karte hain, CPU-bound mein nahi — uske liye multiprocessing use karo.',
    detailedAnswer: 'The Global Interpreter Lock serialises bytecode execution in CPython. It is released during blocking I/O, so threads and asyncio give real concurrency for network and disk work. For CPU-bound work threads give you nothing, because only one runs at a time — use multiprocessing, a native extension that releases the GIL, or a different runtime. The related production trap is calling a blocking function (time.sleep, requests.get, a synchronous ORM) inside an async def, which stalls the entire event loop rather than just that coroutine.',
    detailedAnswerHi: 'Global Interpreter Lock CPython mein bytecode execution ko ek-ek karke chalata hai. Blocking I/O ke dauraan ye chhod diya jata hai, isliye threads aur asyncio network aur disk ke kaam mein asli concurrency dete hain. CPU-bound kaam mein threads se kuch nahi milta, kyunki ek hi chalta hai — multiprocessing, GIL chhodne wala native extension, ya doosra runtime use karo. Isi se juda production trap hai async def ke andar blocking function call karna (time.sleep, requests.get, synchronous ORM), jo sirf us coroutine ko nahi balki poori event loop ko rok deta hai.',
